  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported Indonesia improving climate change adaptation and disaster resilience through the North Java Flood Management Project.  Australia played a key role in preparing the Flood Management in North Java Project, approved in December 2023, with funding from Asian Development Bank and the UK-ASEAN Catalytic Green Finance Facility Trust Fund. The project aims to enhance climate resilience in the Cimanuk-Cisanggarung and Seluna river basins, reducing flood risk for 485,000 people, including 200,000 poor and vulnerable individuals, and minimizing crop and asset damage. It will also mitigate flood risks and traffic disruptions on the main road connecting Jakarta to East Java, benefiting local and national economies.
&#x200d;
The project will operationalize flood risk management by establishing earth observation services and flood forecasting and warning systems, enhancing regional planning and land zoning, and increasing the climate resilience of flood protection infrastructure.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia through Integrated Participatory Development and Management of Irrigation Program (IPDMIP) has strengthened women-led Water User Associations (WUAs), including one in Tengker, Lombok district. This support enabled better planning of cropping and irrigation management to meet seasonal water needs. The WUA formed Women Cares River Groups to monitor canal conditions and collect user fees across seven locations in the Bongoh Tengker irrigation system.
&#x200d;
IPDMIP also helped pilot an Integrated Farming System, including rice intensification and crop diversification. Through demonstration plots, the program enhanced women&apos;s skills in using superior seeds, improved tillage, fertilization, irrigation management, and pest control. As a result, farm productivity increased from 2-3 tons/ha to 4-8 tons/ha per harvest, boosting incomes by around 5%. In 2023, the Asian Development Bank Board of Directors visited the project and interacted with the local community.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2023-24, Australia provided support to the ADB-financed Emergency Assistance for Rehabilitation and Reconstruction: Transport Component project in Indonesia. The project, with a USD109.75 million loan from the ADB and a closing date of March 2024, aimed to rehabilitate and reconstruct critical infrastructure in central Sulawesi damaged by the 2018 earthquake. This involved the rehabilitation and reconstruction of three ports (Wani, Pantoloan and Donggala) and one airport (Mutiara Sis Al Jufri) to disaster-resilient standards with inclusive features. 
Australia&#x2019;s support included technical assistance, procurement, contract administration, environmental and social safeguards, and gender and disability aspects. Key contributions included facilitating the approval of social and environmental safeguards documents and ensuring accessible infrastructure designs for people with disability.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Limiting Environmental Impacts in Indonesia&#x2019;s New Capital
&#x200d;
At the request of the Nusantara Capital Authority (NCA), Australia has prioritised resources to minimize the environmental impacts of developing the Nusantara, Indonesia&#x2019;s new capital.  Australia&#x2019;s strategic environmental assessment produced several key outputs:
&#x200d;
&#x2022;Nusantara Net Zero Strategy: Launched during COP 28 in December 2023, this roadmap outlines NCA&#x2019;s plan to achieve net zero by 2045 through climate mitigation and adaptation actions.
&#x200d;
&#x2022;Nusantara Voluntary Local Review Baseline for Sustainable Development Goals: Monitors and evaluates development against 24 key performance indicators and 17 goals mandated by the Nusantara Law and global urban agenda.
&#x200d;
&#x2022;Nusantara Biodiversity Management Masterplan: Maps the region&apos;s biodiversity, identifying seven areas requiring the highest protection during development.
&#x200d;
&#x2022;Nusantara Environmental Baseline: Provides a foundational measurement of current environmental quality, aiding NCA in maintaining water, soil, and air quality over time.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
